From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jeff Queisser <funkzy@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 09:19:09 -0800
Subject: [PATCH 05/12] iommu/apple-dart: Fix the T6050 display co-processor
 failing buffer setup

On the M5 Ultra Mac Studio (J775D) the display co-processor's start
failed its first buffer requests: the log ring and crash log it had
placed itself resolved to address 0, so RTKit could not read them, one
of the two failures that kept the display down. Those buffers now
resolve, and Linux never places a mapping in the firmware's slots.

A co-processor behind a locked four-level root can place buffers at DVAs
of its own, in the root slots firmware holds. T6050's DCP does: its log
ring lies in its shared data segment and its crash log in its shared
segment, both mapped by firmware before Linux starts. apple-dart never
looked behind those slots, so iova_to_phys() returned 0 for those DVAs
and RTKit could not read either buffer.

With fw_tree set, mapping a locked root records the subtrees behind
firmware's slots (each root and mid-level entry and a mapping of the
table it points to), and iova_to_phys() resolves a DVA in those slots
through them. Leaf entries are read live, and an entry that no longer
matches what was recorded resolves nothing. The slots are also reported
as reserved regions, so the IOVA allocator never places a Linux mapping
there. Linux still never writes into firmware's slots.

The three-level path's reserved-RAM check would refuse these tables:
T6050's boot loader places them outside the memory node. A subtree's
table is mapped if it is reserved RAM or memory that no resource claims
(apple_dart_fw_tree_table()), so an entry that names Linux's own RAM or
a device's registers is refused and its DVAs resolve nothing.

iova_to_phys() now asks the lookup for four-level domains too. Without
a recorded subtree it still finds nothing there: the three-level
snapshot (apple_dart_snapshot_fw_root()) never takes a four-level root.

The host test (tools/asahi/apple-dart-fw-tests.py) takes the five new
functions, the two fields and the subtree's struct, and twelve cases: a
recorded subtree resolves; a changed root or mid-level entry, or no
recorded subtree, resolves nothing; with fw_tree the firmware slot of a
four-level root is reserved, and a fw_mirror DART without fw_tree
neither resolves nor reserves anything there; a subtree freed after a
failed allocation touches only what was allocated; and a subtree table
is taken as reserved RAM or unclaimed memory, never as Linux's RAM, a
claimed range or a misaligned address.

fw_tree is set only for apple,t6050-dart, where it is tested. On T6041,
T8140 and T8142 nothing changes: the reservation walk runs only for the
reserved-table handoff and fw_tree, so their DARTs see no new register
reads. Before widening fw_tree, check whether any of their locked
streams is four-level and what its foreign slots hold, since the
reservation moves DMA placement for every client of that stream.

Signed-off-by: Jeff Queisser <funkzy@users.noreply.github.com>

(cherry picked from commit c07bc8bc78ec280857ea66b27150c4166da538f2)
